+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## SQLite Index Implementation
+
+### Schema
+
+```sql
+CREATE TABLE equipment (
+    serial TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
+    description TEXT NOT NULL,
+    calibration_date TEXT NOT NULL,  -- ISO 8601
+    due_date TEXT NOT NULL           -- ISO 8601
+);
+CREATE INDEX idx_equipment_serial ON equipment(serial);
+```
+
+### Index Building
+
+On valid snapshot import:
+1. Write records to a temporary SQLite file (`registry.new.db`)
+2. Build the index in the temporary file
+3. Validate record count matches expected count
+4. Atomically replace the active database (`registry.db`) via rename
+5. Update the active snapshot date in the metadata table
+
+### Activation
+
+The active database file is `registry.db` in the registry data directory. A metadata table stores the snapshot date and schema version:
+
+```sql
+CREATE TABLE metadata (
+    key TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
+    value TEXT NOT NULL
+);
+-- Keys: 'snapshot_date', 'schema_version'
+```
+
+Lookup opens `registry.db` read-only. The snapshot date is read from metadata on each lookup to satisfy REG-002.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory, Managed search service | HLD tenet requires all lookup to complete using files on the kiosk with no runtime network access. SQLite satisfies this natively; the other two options require network connectivity that kiosks do not have during normal operation. |
+| Index rebuild strategy | Full rebuild on each snapshot import | Incremental updates, delta patches | Snapshots are full replacements (90K records, 150 MB). Full rebuild is simpler, atomic, and completes within the import window. Incremental logic adds complexity for no operational benefit since snapshots arrive infrequently. |
+| Database activation | Atomic file rename after validation | In-place migration, dual-database read | Rename is atomic on POSIX; a failed import never leaves a partially written active database. The previous `registry.db` remains usable until the rename succeeds. |
